Thanks for the PR, @varshavaradarajan! I have a few suggestions.

* The problem seems to be limited to Eviction. Because the only Eviction causes invokes the fake reactor for "create" ([code](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/e0b2787ee1e066287d2d19c3841f18f07669b47d/staging/src/k8s.io/client-go/kubernetes/typed/core/v1/fake/fake_pod_expansion.go#L81-L91)). If so, can you limit the fix to Eviction?
*  The code in fixture.go should not be resource-specific. If we only want to fix the Eviction, we can make a patch in the Eviction [method](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/e0b2787ee1e066287d2d19c3841f18f07669b47d/staging/src/k8s.io/client-go/kubernetes/typed/core/v1/fake/fake_pod_expansion.go#L81-L91). Maybe something like this:

```golang
func (c *FakePods) Evict(ctx context.Context, eviction *policy.Eviction) error {
	action := core.CreateActionImpl{}
	action.Verb = "create"
	action.Namespace = c.ns
	action.Resource = podsResource
	action.Subresource = "eviction"
       pod := v1.Pod{
          // copy the namespace, name etc. from the eviction object
          ObjectMeta: eviction.ObjectMeta
       }
	action.Object = pod

	_, err := c.Fake.Invokes(action, eviction)
	return err
}
```

@caesarxuchao -

```
func (c *FakePods) Evict(ctx context.Context, eviction *policy.Eviction) error {
	action := core.CreateActionImpl{}
	action.Verb = "create"
	action.Namespace = c.ns
	action.Resource = podsResource
	action.Subresource = "eviction"
       pod := v1.Pod{
          // copy the namespace, name etc. from the eviction object
          ObjectMeta: eviction.ObjectMeta
       }
	action.Object = pod

	_, err := c.Fake.Invokes(action, eviction)
	return err
}
```

Won't this mean we are creating another pod object instead of attaching the subresource to the existing pod? If we don't want to handle specific resources in fixture.go and if we don't want to save the subresource, then maybe we don't do `CreateActionImpl` at all for `Binding` or `Eviction` ? WDYT?

issue_context.body:
The client-go Kubernetes fake client can’t list pods after an eviction is created.


**What happened**:

https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/71599 introduced a regression in the fake clientset, where creating an Eviction causes listing pods to fail with `item[0]: can't assign or convert v1beta1.Eviction into v1.Pod`. This is because the Eviction is stored next to the pods, so listing the pods also lists the evictions which then fail to get assigned to the PodList.

**What you expected to happen**:

Listing pods to work after creating an eviction

**How to reproduce it (as minimally and precisely as possible)**:

```

package main

import (
        v1 "k8s.io/api/core/v1"
        policy "k8s.io/api/policy/v1beta1"
        meta_v1 "k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/apis/meta/v1"
        "k8s.io/client-go/kubernetes/fake"
)

func main() {
        client := fake.NewSimpleClientset(
                &v1.Pod{
                        ObjectMeta: meta_v1.ObjectMeta{
                                Name:      "pod-1",
                                Namespace: "default",
                        },
                },
        )
        client.CoreV1().Pods("default").Evict(&policy.Eviction{
                ObjectMeta: meta_v1.ObjectMeta{
                        Name: "pod-1",
                },
        })
        _, err := client.CoreV1().Pods("default").List(meta_v1.ListOptions{})
        // err: item[0]: can't assign or convert v1beta1.Eviction into v1.Pod
}
```

**Anything else we need to know?**:

To fix this we could filter out the resources that have a ‘subresource’ when listing, or not store the evictions at all.

**Environment**:
- client-go version: `k8s.io/client-go v0.0.0-20190620085101-78d2af792bab`
